From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: GloriousEggroll <gloriouseggroll@gmail.com>
Date: Sun, 6 Sep 2026 14:00:00 -0600
Subject: [PATCH] win32u: Release windows abandoned by terminated threads

Lineage II's NSplash.dll terminates its splash thread and unloads without
successfully destroying the thread's window. The server removes the HWND,
but TerminateThread does not run USER32's thread detach callback. The native
window and local rendering resources consequently survive.

Notify surviving GUI queues when server teardown removes a terminated
thread's window. Retain a list of local window objects independently of
the shared server handle table so an invalidated or reused slot does not
make the old WND unreachable. Claim the exact full HWND once under the
USER lock, then release driver and surface resources outside that lock,
using the same resource cleanup as normal thread detach.

Do not call application window procedures or DLL_THREAD_DETACH on behalf
of the terminated thread, and do not clean up from its signal handler.
The surviving queues receive an internal notification, not an application
message. Handle-slot reuse must not clear a replacement client object.

Allow X11 window destruction from a surviving thread which has not yet
initialized its own display data. This is shared USER cleanup, not a
Wayland-only or executable-specific rule.

This does not change DLL_PROCESS_DETACH or bypass C++ mutexes. The separate
ncplatformsdk-cpp.dll shutdown wait observed in Lineage II still needs
investigation/comparison with the native C++ runtime.

---
